1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,160
The distinctive sound of its lever action echoes across the generations.

2
00:00:05,860 --> 00:00:11,600
Its name still evokes images of the Wild West, cowboys, Indians, and the taming of the frontier.

3
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:16,020
For lawmen and outlaw alike, this was the rifle of choice in the Old West.

4
00:00:16,420 --> 00:00:19,080
It was fast, powerful, and deadly accurate.

5
00:00:19,540 --> 00:00:22,100
Now, the Winchester on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,840
The gun has played a critical role in history,

7
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:39,760
an invention which has been praised and denounced,

8
00:00:40,140 --> 00:00:41,740
served hero and villain alike,

9
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:44,380
and carries with it moral responsibility.

10
00:00:44,760 --> 00:00:48,500
To understand the gun is to better understand history.

11
00:00:50,880 --> 00:00:53,880
No other gun looks quite like the Winchester.

12
00:00:54,900 --> 00:00:58,200
Generations have grown up with the image of that lever-action rifle

13
00:00:58,201 --> 00:01:05,560
held in the hands of such celebrated figures as Buffalo Bill Cody, Annie Oakley, and Billy the Kid.

14
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:14,980
The big advantage of the early Winchesters over previous weaponry is the fact that firepower, pure and simple.

15
00:01:15,780 --> 00:01:21,620
They were lever action, which made possible the use of a magazine to carry extra ammunition.

16
00:01:22,320 --> 00:01:27,440
It just made it a practical way to store, carry, and utilize your firepower.

17
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:33,560
For outlaw and lawman alike, this was the rifle of choice in the Old West.

18
00:01:33,561 --> 00:01:37,560
It was fast, powerful and deadly accurate.

19
00:01:43,560 --> 00:01:49,040
During his days as a hunter and a rancher, Theodore Roosevelt relied on its rapid fire ability.

20
00:01:49,041 --> 00:01:52,240
He called it the best weapon he ever had.

21
00:01:52,241 --> 00:02:02,960
It is, of course, the Winchester that is familiar to anyone who has ever seen John Wayne swing his rifle up in movies like Stagecoach and True Grit.

22
00:02:04,580 --> 00:02:09,460
And it is the object of James Stewart's pursuit in his movie Winchester 73.

23
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:14,420
Seems they knew all about your Springfield being single shot.

24
00:02:15,780 --> 00:02:16,820
You mean they had repeaters?

25
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:17,700
Yeah.

26
00:02:17,860 --> 00:02:20,320
Only this time we just might outfox them.

27
00:02:20,321 --> 00:02:22,920
I kind of, we got two Winchesters.

28
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:26,840
But long before it was a movie prop,

29
00:02:27,080 --> 00:02:30,080
the Winchester repeating rifle was an indispensable tool

30
00:02:30,081 --> 00:02:31,820
in a dangerous and remote frontier.

31
00:02:32,740 --> 00:02:35,520
It was a lifesaver and a way to put meat on the table.

32
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:38,300
Like the axe, the horse, and the wagon wheel,

33
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:40,460
the Winchester was a necessity.

34
00:02:41,700 --> 00:02:45,680
The story of the Winchester begins with the opening of the American West.

35
00:02:46,280 --> 00:02:49,460
In the 1850s, before the repeating rifle was invented,

36
00:02:49,461 --> 00:02:54,020
pioneers carried single-shot rifles like those made by the Sharps company.

37
00:02:56,220 --> 00:03:01,020
The Sharps traveled with many wagon trains from the Mississippi to the Rio Grande.

38
00:03:02,080 --> 00:03:05,820
It also convinced Native Americans like the Cheyenne and the Sioux

39
00:03:05,821 --> 00:03:09,460
that bows and arrows could never stop the advance of white settlers.

40
00:03:12,660 --> 00:03:18,020
But while the Sharps was a great improvement over the muzzle-loading rifles and muskets that it replaced,

41
00:03:18,021 --> 00:03:25,260
it still only fired a single shot and then had to be reloaded.

42
00:03:26,060 --> 00:03:28,920
In the ten seconds it took to complete this task,

43
00:03:29,340 --> 00:03:31,900
a wounded animal could strike back or get away,

44
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:34,920
and an Indian warrior could land a death blow.

45
00:03:37,220 --> 00:03:41,360
The Winchester changed the shape of battle

46
00:03:41,361 --> 00:03:46,700
in the sense that with a single shot muzzle loading

47
00:03:46,701 --> 00:03:51,500
or even a breech-loading arm, it just took a lot longer in between shots.

48
00:03:51,820 --> 00:03:55,440
But as long as you had ammunition in the magazine of the Winchester,

49
00:03:55,700 --> 00:03:57,000
there was nothing that could beat it.

50
00:03:57,640 --> 00:04:02,120
The development of the repeating rifle began with New York City inventor Walter Hunt.

51
00:04:02,840 --> 00:04:07,020
Hunt had already invented the safety pin, the lock-stitched needle, and the fountain pen.

52
00:04:07,820 --> 00:04:12,480
In 1848, he patented a loaded bullet, which he called the rocket ball.

53
00:04:13,440 --> 00:04:15,660
It's essentially just a lead ball.

54
00:04:15,661 --> 00:04:20,400
but when you turn it, you can see that it's got a plug in the base.

55
00:04:21,440 --> 00:04:24,660
That plug is covering some black powder

56
00:04:24,661 --> 00:04:28,680
and a very small disk of fulminate of mercury.

57
00:04:30,700 --> 00:04:34,080
Hunt's next step was to design a gun that fired his rockets

58
00:04:34,081 --> 00:04:36,040
one after another without stopping.

59
00:04:36,860 --> 00:04:40,840
In 1849, he was granted a patent for a repeating firearm

60
00:04:40,841 --> 00:04:43,720
that held the ammunition in a tube under the barrel.

61
00:04:44,420 --> 00:04:47,240
He called his contraption the volitional repeater.

62
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:53,880
The heart of the volitional repeater was a lever-activated mechanism.

63
00:04:54,740 --> 00:04:59,300
When cocked, it brought the bullets from the tube up into the chamber, ready for firing.

64
00:05:01,200 --> 00:05:05,920
While the design of this forerunner of the Winchester was sound, it did have its shortcomings.

65
00:05:06,320 --> 00:05:11,520
The patent called for small, delicate parts that made the gun troublesome to build and operate.

66
00:05:11,521 --> 00:05:18,520
Another serious fault was the size of the lever, which was only big enough for one finger to operate it.

67
00:05:21,520 --> 00:05:27,520
Because Hunt lacked the funds to promote his invention, the volitional repeater was never mass-produced.

68
00:05:27,521 --> 00:05:34,520
The only known example is housed today in the Winchester Collection at the Cody Firearms Museum in Cody, Wyoming.

69
00:05:34,521 --> 00:05:44,020
Hunt's basic design was later improved on by a talented gunsmith named Lewis Jennings.

70
00:05:44,021 --> 00:05:48,400
Jennings made the gun more reliable by simplifying the repeating mechanism.

71
00:05:48,401 --> 00:05:56,120
The result was patented on Christmas Day 1849 and called the Jennings rifle.

72
00:05:56,121 --> 00:06:00,840
The rifle's long barrel was made of wood and it retained the small finger loop lever of

73
00:06:00,841 --> 00:06:03,520
its predecessor.

74
00:06:03,521 --> 00:06:05,260
The Jennings was a commercial failure.

75
00:06:05,860 --> 00:06:10,960
It was awkward, and its ammunition was not powerful enough for big game or long-range firing.

76
00:06:11,760 --> 00:06:14,680
Only 1,000 were made before production was halted.

77
00:06:18,940 --> 00:06:23,100
No one knew it at the time, but the basic design and layout of this failure

78
00:06:23,101 --> 00:06:27,600
would be carried forward into one of the most successful guns the world has ever known.

79
00:06:27,601 --> 00:06:34,880
Though it didn't look much like the Winchester familiar in the hands of cowboys and lawmen,

80
00:06:34,881 --> 00:06:40,360
this was the great grandfather of the gun that won the West.

81
00:06:40,361 --> 00:06:44,980
But before the name Winchester came to be associated with it, there would be more improvement

82
00:06:44,981 --> 00:06:46,720
and innovation.

83
00:06:46,721 --> 00:06:54,400
This would come from a legendary pair of gun makers known by the names Smith and Wesson.

84
00:06:54,401 --> 00:07:01,400
Their contribution promised to be nothing short of volcanic.

85
00:07:03,400 --> 00:07:08,580
Buffalo Bill claimed he once used his Winchester rifle to put 11 bullets into the stomach of

86
00:07:08,581 --> 00:07:12,160
a bear that had been charging him from 30 yards away.

87
00:07:12,161 --> 00:07:18,900
The Winchester on Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

88
00:07:18,901 --> 00:07:23,500
Life was tough for those brave enough to venture into the West in the 1850s.

89
00:07:23,501 --> 00:07:27,880
Their search for a better life brought them face to face with hostile native people and

90
00:07:27,881 --> 00:07:32,740
an unforgiving wilderness.

91
00:07:32,741 --> 00:07:37,380
But if Americans had been content with weapons from the past, their westward migration might

92
00:07:37,381 --> 00:07:41,140
have stalled somewhere between Ohio and Missouri.

93
00:07:41,141 --> 00:07:44,860
In the 1850s, an advanced weapon was being developed.

94
00:07:44,861 --> 00:07:48,320
This was the repeating rifle.

95
00:07:48,321 --> 00:07:51,000
First there had been the one-of-a-kind hunt repeater.

96
00:07:51,001 --> 00:07:54,600
successor the Jennings rifle went into production at the Robins and Lawrence

97
00:07:54,601 --> 00:07:59,100
factory in Vermont although it failed to attract a following it did get the

98
00:07:59,101 --> 00:08:05,460
attention of two gunsmiths at the factory those two were none other than Horace

99
00:08:05,461 --> 00:08:11,640
Smith and Daniel Wesson Wesson an experienced gunsmith was working at the

100
00:08:11,641 --> 00:08:15,700
Robins and Lawrence factory in 1850 when he had begun experimenting with the

101
00:08:15,701 --> 00:08:20,580
failed Jennings rifle.

102
00:08:20,581 --> 00:08:24,640
At about the same time, Horace Smith was hired at the factory to further develop

103
00:08:24,641 --> 00:08:26,340
the Jennings rifle.

104
00:08:26,341 --> 00:08:32,740
Smith was an established arms maker and had his own shop in Norwich, Connecticut.

105
00:08:32,741 --> 00:08:36,540
From their work came a new patent for a repeating firearm.

106
00:08:36,541 --> 00:08:41,520
It was granted on February 14, 1854.

107
00:08:41,521 --> 00:08:44,880
Next, the two formed a partnership and called the new business

108
00:08:44,881 --> 00:08:47,440
Smith & Wesson.

109
00:08:47,441 --> 00:08:50,120
Manufacturing began at Smith's shop.

110
00:08:50,121 --> 00:08:57,920
To start, the company made handguns that relied on the newly designed lever action mechanism.

111
00:08:57,921 --> 00:09:04,500
With the Smith & Wesson design, we finally have a lever held by two fingers and in terms

112
00:09:04,501 --> 00:09:09,120
of the rifle by the full hand that swings forward.

113
00:09:09,121 --> 00:09:13,760
And this is of course the lever action that we associate with all Winchester lever actions

114
00:09:13,761 --> 00:09:21,760
from there on.

115
00:09:21,761 --> 00:09:26,820
Smith and Wesson's design offered the ability to load as many as 30 bullets into the magazine

116
00:09:26,821 --> 00:09:28,520
under the barrel.

117
00:09:28,521 --> 00:09:32,640
Its rapid fire capability suggested a dramatic trade name.

118
00:09:32,641 --> 00:09:38,280
The new firearm was christened the Volcanic.

119
00:09:38,281 --> 00:09:42,500
With the addition of a shoulder stock onto the handle of this long barreled pistol, the

120
00:09:42,501 --> 00:09:47,240
volcanic looked almost like a rifle. The evolution toward the Winchester of the

121
00:09:47,241 --> 00:09:50,460
future had begun.

122
00:09:53,220 --> 00:09:57,160
Smith and Wesson claimed that their volcanic could be loaded in less than a

123
00:09:57,161 --> 00:10:01,700
minute and that its ammunition was waterproof. Its proportions were said to

124
00:10:01,701 --> 00:10:08,620
be light, compact and even elegant. The workmanship was advertised as perfection.

125
00:10:08,621 --> 00:10:13,620
By 1855, Smith and Wesson were looking for investors.

126
00:10:13,621 --> 00:10:19,620
This iron-framed lever action repeater was produced by the firm as a demonstration sample.

127
00:10:19,621 --> 00:10:24,620
Richly engraved, they hoped it would promote business and attract venture capital.

128
00:10:24,621 --> 00:10:30,620
With this prototype, Smith and Wesson had once again crafted the shape of things to come.

129
00:10:30,621 --> 00:10:39,220
I don't think we can overestimate how much impact they had on the final design of the

130
00:10:39,221 --> 00:10:46,560
Winchester rifle because their 1854 patent and the way they manufactured the gun carries

131
00:10:46,561 --> 00:10:52,380
primary designs that stay with us for years and years with the Winchester.

132
00:10:52,381 --> 00:10:57,300
To further promote the business to investors, Smith & Wesson renamed their firm the Volcanic

133
00:10:57,301 --> 00:10:59,460
Repeating Arms Company.

134
00:10:59,461 --> 00:11:05,160
40 local businessmen, including clockmakers, grocers, bakers and shoemakers, bought in

135
00:11:05,161 --> 00:11:10,820
hoping to get rich on the prospect of repeating rifles.

136
00:11:10,821 --> 00:11:15,340
One of the new backers was a successful shirt manufacturer from New Haven, Connecticut,

137
00:11:15,341 --> 00:11:18,060
who catered to an upscale clientele.

138
00:11:18,061 --> 00:11:20,940
He bought 80 shares at $25 each.

139
00:11:20,941 --> 00:11:25,700
His name was Oliver Winchester.

140
00:11:25,701 --> 00:11:31,940
When Oliver Winchester invested in the Volcanic Arms Company, he knew absolutely nothing about

141
00:11:31,941 --> 00:11:33,160
firearms.

142
00:11:33,161 --> 00:11:37,540
To him it was a mere investment.

143
00:11:37,541 --> 00:11:40,200
Winchester was born in Boston in 1810.

144
00:11:40,201 --> 00:11:44,100
At the age of seven, young Oliver went to work on a farm.

145
00:11:44,101 --> 00:11:49,700
He attended school in the winter when it was too cold to work outdoors.

146
00:11:49,701 --> 00:11:54,020
By the age of 20, Winchester had become a skilled carpenter.

147
00:11:54,021 --> 00:11:58,340
He moved to Baltimore, Maryland, where he supervised the construction of numerous buildings and

148
00:11:58,341 --> 00:12:03,080
homes, as well as a church.

149
00:12:03,081 --> 00:12:09,760
At 24, Winchester moved to New Haven, Connecticut and opened a men's clothing store.

150
00:12:09,761 --> 00:12:13,860
Later that year, he began to manufacture a line of men's shirts that were cut with an

151
00:12:13,861 --> 00:12:15,740
improved pattern.

152
00:12:15,741 --> 00:12:22,200
In 1848, he patented this design and opened a factory in New Haven, Connecticut.

153
00:12:22,201 --> 00:12:30,600
The business was lucrative, and by 1855 the company grossed $600,000 per year.

154
00:12:30,601 --> 00:12:34,140
Oliver Winchester is a strange duck.

155
00:12:34,141 --> 00:12:36,160
He's a man who's growing up in New England.

156
00:12:36,161 --> 00:12:38,600
He's mainly a shirt manufacturer.

157
00:12:38,601 --> 00:12:42,960
He gets his money with that, but he knows enough to realize that firearms are making

158
00:12:42,961 --> 00:12:44,620
tremendous strides.

159
00:12:44,621 --> 00:12:49,400
And he gets into the ground floor of that by investing in the Volcanic Repeating Arms

160
00:12:49,401 --> 00:12:51,700
Company.

161
00:12:51,701 --> 00:12:55,400
the company that Oliver Winchester had invested in was heading for trouble.

162
00:12:56,440 --> 00:13:01,580
The volcanics, of course, had the great novelty effect of being a repeating system.

163
00:13:01,680 --> 00:13:04,160
You could just crank out a lot of shots in a hurry.

164
00:13:04,680 --> 00:13:11,440
But the cartridge that they used, which was a hollow-based lead bullet with a very small powder charge,

165
00:13:11,900 --> 00:13:17,400
could not propel the projectile to any great distance or with any kind of hitting effect.

166
00:13:17,401 --> 00:13:20,340
The idea was great, but it was a commercial flop.

167
00:13:21,700 --> 00:13:26,700
Sales of the Volcanics were poor and the company was falling into debt.

168
00:13:26,701 --> 00:13:29,700
Most of the investors pulled out, even Smith & Wesson.

169
00:13:29,701 --> 00:13:35,700
They wanted to concentrate on developing the revolvers that would someday make their names legendary.

170
00:13:36,700 --> 00:13:39,700
Winchester was left in control.

171
00:13:39,701 --> 00:13:46,700
By 1856, he was both president and treasurer of the Volcanic Repeating Arms Company.

172
00:13:46,701 --> 00:13:50,700
Although the problems with the guns and ammunition weren't going away,

173
00:13:50,701 --> 00:13:54,580
Winchester had the idea that a name change might help solve these issues.

174
00:13:55,440 --> 00:14:00,380
In 1857, the Volcanic Arms Company became the New Haven Arms Company.

175
00:14:01,980 --> 00:14:05,500
The factory employed a workforce of 50 machinists and gunsmiths,

176
00:14:06,160 --> 00:14:09,040
as well as a team of women who worked in the ammunition department.

177
00:14:09,900 --> 00:14:12,640
The man in the window was Oliver Winchester.

178
00:14:15,120 --> 00:14:18,880
In advertising, Winchester made early use of testimonials

179
00:14:18,881 --> 00:14:21,200
to persuade dealers to carry his firearms.

180
00:14:22,100 --> 00:14:25,880
An 1859 broadside boasted these firsthand reports.

181
00:14:28,580 --> 00:14:31,260
Gentlemen, I consider the volcanic repeating pistol

182
00:14:31,261 --> 00:14:33,420
to be the highest point of repeating arms.

183
00:14:33,780 --> 00:14:37,380
It has no equal and excels in rapidity, efficiency,

184
00:14:37,620 --> 00:14:39,280
and certainty of execution.

185
00:14:40,220 --> 00:14:43,260
Its ball is waterproof and cannot be damaged

186
00:14:43,261 --> 00:14:44,780
by any change of climate,

187
00:14:44,781 --> 00:14:47,720
and is sure fire even after having been loaded

188
00:14:47,721 --> 00:14:49,200
for a trip around the world.

189
00:14:51,140 --> 00:14:53,660
In spite of small pockets of enthusiasm

190
00:14:53,661 --> 00:14:55,620
for Winchester's repeating arms,

191
00:14:56,120 --> 00:14:57,180
sales remained low.

192
00:14:57,880 --> 00:14:59,880
His guns were hobbled by weak ammunition,

193
00:15:00,340 --> 00:15:01,740
and unless that was improved,

194
00:15:02,040 --> 00:15:03,240
the business was doomed.

195
00:15:04,540 --> 00:15:07,000
As rumors of civil war circulated

196
00:15:07,001 --> 00:15:09,680
and the market for firearms out west expanded,

197
00:15:10,380 --> 00:15:13,080
Winchester raced to develop the perfect gun and cartridge.

198
00:15:13,081 --> 00:15:17,360
It would be powerful, dependable, and profitable.

199
00:15:17,720 --> 00:15:31,720
A century after the Model 94 was introduced in 1894, roughly 6 million of these celebrated

200
00:15:33,780 --> 00:15:36,212
Winchester rifles were sold across America.

201
00:15:36,213 --> 00:15:38,700
The Winchester on Modern Marvels will return

202
00:15:38,701 --> 00:15:39,401
in a moment.

203
00:15:39,402 --> 00:15:50,800
In 1857, Oliver Winchester had taken over a failing firearms business, hoping to turn it around.

204
00:15:52,940 --> 00:15:54,660
Winchester faced two challenges.

205
00:15:55,200 --> 00:16:00,620
He needed to develop a more powerful bullet, and he needed a gun that could reliably fire it.

206
00:16:00,621 --> 00:16:11,860
Winchester was a new type of entrepreneur in the firearms industry.

207
00:16:11,861 --> 00:16:17,340
Up to this time, prominent figures such as Eli Whitney, Eliafit Remington and Sam Colt

208
00:16:17,341 --> 00:16:24,420
had been investors and skilled machinists who had experience with firearms as well.

209
00:16:24,421 --> 00:16:30,580
But Winchester entered the trade with only a background in shirt making.

210
00:16:30,581 --> 00:16:35,220
It was in fact from his shirt business that Oliver Winchester found the designer he needed

211
00:16:35,221 --> 00:16:38,500
to solve his problems and make history.

212
00:16:38,501 --> 00:16:44,380
In 1857, Winchester hired Benjamin Tyler Henry to be plant superintendent of the New Haven

213
00:16:44,381 --> 00:16:48,880
Arms Company.

214
00:16:48,881 --> 00:16:54,300
Henry had been master mechanic at the Winchester Shirt Manufacturing Company in the 1850s.

215
00:16:54,301 --> 00:16:59,400
There, he repaired and maintained the 500 foot pedal sewing machines that were in use

216
00:16:59,401 --> 00:17:00,500
on the production floor.

217
00:17:02,800 --> 00:17:05,400
Henry had apprenticed as a gunsmith when he was 16

218
00:17:05,401 --> 00:17:08,360
and had worked in various New England gun shops

219
00:17:08,361 --> 00:17:10,320
as well as at the Springfield Armory.

220
00:17:12,680 --> 00:17:14,840
Under Henry, manufacturing continued

221
00:17:14,841 --> 00:17:16,600
on the old volcanic repeaters

222
00:17:16,601 --> 00:17:19,480
while he experimented with new cartridge designs

223
00:17:19,481 --> 00:17:21,180
and an improved repeating rifle.

224
00:17:23,240 --> 00:17:24,720
According to his memoirs,

225
00:17:24,721 --> 00:17:28,160
Henry lived in the shop sleeping just an hour or two at a time.

226
00:17:28,161 --> 00:17:34,360
Day and night were the same to him, and rumors of war pushed him to perfect a gun and cartridge

227
00:17:34,361 --> 00:17:41,260
that would be dependable and attractive to the Army.

228
00:17:41,261 --> 00:17:47,660
When Henry developed his rifle, it was sort of the acme of firearms development at the

229
00:17:47,661 --> 00:17:49,160
time.

230
00:17:49,161 --> 00:17:53,640
In design and function, it had no equal.

231
00:17:53,641 --> 00:17:57,580
Winchester realized that the future, if the rifle was to make as many as possible and

232
00:17:57,581 --> 00:18:03,400
sell them as quickly as possible for federal use.

233
00:18:03,401 --> 00:18:08,200
The rifle Henry developed would mark a turning point in firearms history.

234
00:18:08,201 --> 00:18:12,780
Building on the foundation of the volcanic mechanism designed by Smith & Wesson, Henry

235
00:18:12,781 --> 00:18:17,920
designed a repeating rifle that fired larger, more powerful ammunition.

236
00:18:17,921 --> 00:18:22,360
It was called the Henry Repeating Rifle.

237
00:18:22,361 --> 00:18:25,480
Its ease of use and reliability were unparalleled.

238
00:18:25,481 --> 00:18:29,680
All you had to do was throw this lever.

239
00:18:29,681 --> 00:18:35,480
And when the lever came back, the hammer was already cocked and the new cartridge was fresh

240
00:18:35,481 --> 00:18:36,480
in the breech.

241
00:18:36,481 --> 00:18:41,980
The magazine was right underneath the barrel fed by this spring.

242
00:18:41,981 --> 00:18:46,860
And this was the first practical lever action firearm ever made.

243
00:18:47,920 --> 00:18:55,920
B. Tyler Henry patented the rifle in 1860 and granted its ownership to Winchester's

244
00:18:55,921 --> 00:19:00,027
New Haven Arms Company. Production began there in 1861.

245
00:19:00,028 --> 00:19:02,640
At the same time, the factory began

246
00:19:02,641 --> 00:19:06,000
making the new cartridge Henry had made for it.

247
00:19:06,001 --> 00:19:15,360
Henry's primary improvement to the cartridge design was the development of a .44 caliber

248
00:19:15,361 --> 00:19:18,360
self-contained metallic cartridge.

249
00:19:18,361 --> 00:19:22,100
The powder is contained in a copper shell.

250
00:19:22,101 --> 00:19:25,080
The bullet is placed at the top of that shell.

251
00:19:26,160 --> 00:19:28,260
When it was fired, the bullet would move forward

252
00:19:28,261 --> 00:19:29,760
through the barrel.

253
00:19:29,761 --> 00:19:32,340
The shell casing itself would remain in the breach

254
00:19:32,341 --> 00:19:34,640
of the barrel and then could be extracted from it.

255
00:19:35,680 --> 00:19:37,440
The design of the metallic cartridges

256
00:19:37,441 --> 00:19:39,420
made them easier to carry.

257
00:19:39,421 --> 00:19:42,320
They could be stored loosely in a box without breakage

258
00:19:42,321 --> 00:19:45,000
or loss of a fragile cork stopper.

259
00:19:45,001 --> 00:19:48,040
And while earlier cartridges claimed to be waterproof,

260
00:19:48,041 --> 00:19:50,840
the Henry cartridges actually were.

261
00:19:50,841 --> 00:19:53,100
Loading was fast and efficient as well.

262
00:19:55,480 --> 00:19:58,580
The front loading Henry magazine

263
00:19:58,581 --> 00:20:02,620
had a slide on a spring,

264
00:20:02,621 --> 00:20:06,420
and you had to pull that up and then twist the barrel back.

265
00:20:06,421 --> 00:20:09,560
That opened the magazine for dropping in the cartridges

266
00:20:09,561 --> 00:20:11,360
with the butt end first.

267
00:20:11,361 --> 00:20:14,400
After you drop them all in, give this a twist,

268
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:16,760
and you're ready to go.

269
00:20:17,860 --> 00:20:21,800
In April 1861, with the firing on Fort Sumter,

270
00:20:22,300 --> 00:20:24,960
America was plunged into its grim Civil War.

271
00:20:25,820 --> 00:20:28,760
Just over a year later, in July of 1862,

272
00:20:29,420 --> 00:20:32,620
the first of Winchester's Henry rifles appeared on the market.

273
00:20:34,520 --> 00:20:37,980
The chief benefit of the Henry rifle during the Civil War

274
00:20:37,981 --> 00:20:41,100
was that it provided an enormous amount of firepower

275
00:20:41,101 --> 00:20:42,700
to anyone who was using it.

276
00:20:42,701 --> 00:20:47,680
You could discharge it 14, 15 times in the space of a minute.

277
00:20:48,940 --> 00:20:56,120
And when you're faced with opposing forces, more likely using single-shot musket,

278
00:20:56,700 --> 00:21:01,020
that meant that one cavalryman or infantryman using a Henry

279
00:21:01,021 --> 00:21:06,240
was the equivalent of 14 or 15 standard infantrymen.

280
00:21:06,241 --> 00:21:13,800
Confederate Colonel John Mosby and his Rangers became infamous for their raids against advanced

281
00:21:13,801 --> 00:21:14,842
Union positions.

282
00:21:14,843 --> 00:21:19,920
When he encountered the Henry in battle, he called it that damned Yankee rifle

283
00:21:19,921 --> 00:21:24,589
that can be loaded on Sunday and fired all week.

284
00:21:24,590 --> 00:21:29,160
It was an awe-inspiring sight and sound for any

285
00:21:29,161 --> 00:21:34,980
soldier who was used to reloading after each shot to be fired on repeatedly by an enemy armed with

286
00:21:34,981 --> 00:21:42,100
such a rifle. In spite of its overwhelming advantages, the Henry was

287
00:21:42,101 --> 00:21:47,040
slow to be accepted by federal ordnance officers, largely because it required a

288
00:21:47,041 --> 00:21:52,380
non-standard ammunition. Its reliability on the battlefield was also unknown.

289
00:21:52,381 --> 00:21:59,360
Still, the Henry was prized by any soldier fortunate enough to own one.

290
00:21:59,361 --> 00:22:04,600
The federal government purchased 1731 and stayed in Kentucky another 50.

291
00:22:04,601 --> 00:22:09,840
However, probably close to 6,000 to 7,000 actually saw service during the war.

292
00:22:10,360 --> 00:22:13,900
These were acquired by individuals who paid for them out of their own pocket,

293
00:22:14,080 --> 00:22:16,960
bought their own ammunition, and served with them in the field.

294
00:22:17,740 --> 00:22:22,060
An average soldier's salary during that period was $13 a month.

295
00:22:22,620 --> 00:22:24,480
A Henry rifle cost $50.

296
00:22:25,640 --> 00:22:31,240
Soldiers like these 7th Illinois Volunteers spent one quarter to one half of their annual income

297
00:22:31,241 --> 00:22:33,420
arming themselves with Henry rifles.

298
00:22:34,600 --> 00:22:40,880
It was a small price for a soldier to pay in order to win a battle and return home with

299
00:22:40,881 --> 00:22:46,720
his life.

300
00:22:46,721 --> 00:22:51,280
This rare surviving Civil War Henry rifle belonged to a Western sharpshooter of the

301
00:22:51,281 --> 00:22:54,200
66th Illinois Regiment.

302
00:22:54,201 --> 00:22:59,400
He was so proud that he had his firearm engraved at extra cost with his name and the name of

303
00:22:59,401 --> 00:23:01,200
his outfit.

304
00:23:01,201 --> 00:23:08,420
Most of the time when firearms are engraved in the Winchester line, it's because the customer

305
00:23:08,421 --> 00:23:10,140
ordered it for himself.

306
00:23:10,141 --> 00:23:14,940
However, among the exceptions, Winchester himself was responsible for a Henry rifle

307
00:23:14,941 --> 00:23:19,100
that was given to President Abraham Lincoln.

308
00:23:19,101 --> 00:23:23,160
Abraham Lincoln was presented with the sixth Henry rifle to come out of the Winchester

309
00:23:23,161 --> 00:23:24,740
factory.

310
00:23:24,741 --> 00:23:30,380
Its frame was plated in gold and intricately engraved and inscribed.

311
00:23:30,381 --> 00:23:35,220
Similar Henry repeaters were also presented to Secretary of War Stanton and Secretary

312
00:23:35,221 --> 00:23:37,520
of the Navy Gideon Wells.

313
00:23:37,521 --> 00:23:43,140
This was not just an act of generosity, it was a shrewd marketing ploy.

314
00:23:43,141 --> 00:23:47,980
This was a time of war and Winchester hoped to acquaint people in power with the benefits

315
00:23:47,981 --> 00:23:51,500
of his new rifles.

316
00:23:51,501 --> 00:23:56,340
Just as important, with presentations like these, Winchester began a sense of legend

317
00:23:56,341 --> 00:24:03,340
for his rifles, a legend that has endured for nearly 150 years.

318
00:24:03,520 --> 00:24:08,960
Since Lincoln's time, presentation Winchester rifles like this one, given to President Eisenhower

319
00:24:08,961 --> 00:24:13,840
in 1960, have remained an American tradition.

320
00:24:13,841 --> 00:24:18,300
Every elected president since Lincoln has been presented with his own inscribed Winchester

321
00:24:18,301 --> 00:24:23,300
rifle by the factory.

322
00:24:23,301 --> 00:24:26,940
In all, 13,000 of the original Henry rifles were made,

323
00:24:27,260 --> 00:24:30,020
and the name became so popular that for a year,

324
00:24:30,260 --> 00:24:32,860
the firm was called the Henry Repeating Rifle Company.

325
00:24:35,040 --> 00:24:38,720
Stamped on the barrel of each rifle made was B. Tyler Henry's name.

326
00:24:41,100 --> 00:24:45,380
But by 1866, Winchester would change the name of his company

327
00:24:45,381 --> 00:24:47,440
and improve his rifle once again.

328
00:24:48,120 --> 00:24:51,760
This time, though, he was to call them both Winchester.

329
00:24:53,300 --> 00:25:02,480
From 1873 to 1889, the height of frontier activity, over 540,000 model 1873s were produced.

330
00:25:03,060 --> 00:25:04,700
The Winchester will continue in a moment.

331
00:25:07,180 --> 00:25:11,980
In the 1860s, life out west promised danger on an open frontier.

332
00:25:12,660 --> 00:25:19,100
Hunting was a means of survival, and survival itself required self-defense against attackers at any moment.

333
00:25:19,101 --> 00:25:24,100
Because of this, the repeating rifle would become a familiar part of the Western landscape.

334
00:25:24,101 --> 00:25:33,100
By 1866, Oliver Winchester's New Haven Arms Company had produced 13,000 lever-action Henry rifles.

335
00:25:33,101 --> 00:25:40,100
His rifle had proven itself in battle during the Civil War, and had become prized by those soldiers lucky enough to carry one.

336
00:25:40,101 --> 00:25:44,100
It had also begun to prove itself in the West.

337
00:25:44,101 --> 00:25:51,100
From Montana came reports that 40 charging Blackfoot Indians were repelled by two prospectors

338
00:25:51,101 --> 00:25:57,100
who were armed with the Henry rifles they had been issued as soldiers during the Civil War.

339
00:25:57,101 --> 00:26:07,100
In Nevada City, three bandits who had robbed a Wells Fargo stagecoach of its cash were killed by four bullets from the Marshalls' Henry Repeater.

340
00:26:07,101 --> 00:26:13,100
Four bullets, three robbers. Impressive to everyone but Oliver Winchester.

341
00:26:13,101 --> 00:26:16,700
Winchester, he believed there was room to refine the Henry.

342
00:26:20,300 --> 00:26:26,300
On July 1st, 1865, Winchester opened a new factory in Bridgeport, Connecticut,

343
00:26:26,301 --> 00:26:28,944
and named it the Winchester Arms Company.

344
00:26:28,945 --> 00:26:32,620
His new order of business was to improve the Henry rifle.

345
00:26:34,140 --> 00:26:40,219
The Henry rifle's major shortcoming was the fact that it had an exposed magazine.

346
00:26:40,220 --> 00:26:41,420
The magazine had

347
00:26:41,421 --> 00:26:44,720
had a slot cut along its lower edge,

348
00:26:44,721 --> 00:26:47,420
which allowed you to load the weapon.

349
00:26:47,421 --> 00:26:50,260
It also allowed every bit of dirt that was around it

350
00:26:50,261 --> 00:26:51,620
to get into it.

351
00:26:51,621 --> 00:26:55,720
And dirt buildup eventually would cause the rifle to fail.

352
00:26:59,420 --> 00:27:01,820
The Henry had two other problems.

353
00:27:01,821 --> 00:27:04,360
During heavy firing, the barrel got hot enough

354
00:27:04,361 --> 00:27:06,420
to burn the rifleman's hand.

355
00:27:06,421 --> 00:27:08,380
And while it was fast, its ammunition

356
00:27:08,381 --> 00:27:12,910
not powerful enough for big game or long-range shooting.

357
00:27:12,911 --> 00:27:15,580
Those problems would be solved by

358
00:27:15,581 --> 00:27:23,742
Winchester's new superintendent Nelson King. King patented the improvement in 1866.

359
00:27:23,743 --> 00:27:25,020
The new rifle

360
00:27:25,021 --> 00:27:28,282
was named the Winchester Model 1866.

361
00:27:28,283 --> 00:27:33,900
The improved Henry had two major features that caused it to be

362
00:27:33,901 --> 00:27:36,300
a major improvement over the earlier Henry.

363
00:27:36,301 --> 00:27:38,140
First was the addition of a wood forearm

364
00:27:38,141 --> 00:27:40,700
to allow you to handle the gun when it got hot.

365
00:27:40,701 --> 00:27:43,340
Secondly was the King's patent loading gate,

366
00:27:43,341 --> 00:27:49,020
which allowed for firing and loading much quicker

367
00:27:49,021 --> 00:27:51,260
without having to move the gun.

368
00:27:54,220 --> 00:27:57,420
Sales of the Model 1866 were strong,

369
00:27:57,421 --> 00:28:02,140
and Winchester produced a total of 170,000 of his improved rifles

370
00:28:02,141 --> 00:28:04,700
to satisfy a growing demand from out west.

371
00:28:05,580 --> 00:28:09,020
The need for the Model 1866 west of the Mississippi

372
00:28:09,021 --> 00:28:10,840
was primarily for defense.

373
00:28:12,160 --> 00:28:15,720
It also could be used for hunting purposes, gathering game.

374
00:28:16,340 --> 00:28:21,760
But emigrants desperately wanted to have that feeling of security

375
00:28:21,761 --> 00:28:24,960
that a Winchester repeating rifle offered them.

376
00:28:28,420 --> 00:28:31,980
With a gleaming new Model 1866 in his hands,

377
00:28:32,140 --> 00:28:38,060
a frontiersman was confident that he could put meat on the table and hold his ground against violent attackers.

378
00:28:43,540 --> 00:28:48,440
Building on the success of his new rifle, Oliver Winchester entered local politics.

379
00:28:49,260 --> 00:28:52,840
In 1866, he was elected Lieutenant Governor of Connecticut.

380
00:28:53,800 --> 00:28:57,740
From then on, he was known as Governor Winchester to friends and associates.

381
00:28:57,741 --> 00:29:05,240
During this time, Winchester lived in grand style in a Victorian home on Prospect Street

382
00:29:05,241 --> 00:29:16,440
in New Haven, Connecticut, with his wife Jane and their children, Anne, Hannah, and William.

383
00:29:16,441 --> 00:29:20,520
Governor Winchester's rifle had become indispensable for pioneers.

384
00:29:20,521 --> 00:29:25,120
It had also gained a following among the Native Americans who acquired them by trade and sometimes

385
00:29:25,121 --> 00:29:26,120
by capture.

386
00:29:26,121 --> 00:29:28,660
They fondly nicknamed it the Yellow Boy.

387
00:29:30,020 --> 00:29:35,340
The Model 1866 Winchester was often called the Yellow Boy due to its brass frame.

388
00:29:35,760 --> 00:29:39,140
American Indians are credited with nicknaming at that.

389
00:29:39,740 --> 00:29:45,600
Because of that bright yellow brass receiver and the brass furniture and other parts of the gun,

390
00:29:45,700 --> 00:29:49,060
it just had a lot of gleaming yellow parts to it, and that appealed to them.

391
00:29:50,220 --> 00:29:55,460
Chief Poundmaker of the Crees tribe was known to carry his prized Yellow Boy on a leather sling.

392
00:29:56,120 --> 00:30:01,860
Other Native Americans decorated their Winchesters with metal tacks hammered into the wood in geometric patterns.

393
00:30:04,960 --> 00:30:14,180
It was the Winchester Model 1866 in the hands of Sioux warriors that confronted General George Custer's troops at the Battle of Little Bighorn.

394
00:30:15,460 --> 00:30:20,400
The General's men were armed with single-shot army rifles made at the Springfield Armory.

395
00:30:20,401 --> 00:30:27,400
Although accurate and deadly, they had to be loaded after each shot and sometimes became fouled under heavy use.

396
00:30:27,401 --> 00:30:36,400
Against the onslaught of repeating fire from those Sioux armed with Winchesters, Custer and his 220 troops were quickly annihilated.

397
00:30:38,400 --> 00:30:42,124
As a military loss, Custer's last stand was a tragedy.

398
00:30:42,125 --> 00:30:46,400
But for the Winchester, it was more fuel for a growing legend.

399
00:30:46,401 --> 00:30:53,400
stagecoach King Ben Holliday chose a Winchester for his personal protection

400
00:30:53,401 --> 00:30:59,040
British explorer Henry Stanley took a Winchester model 1866 along with him on

401
00:30:59,041 --> 00:31:04,920
his expedition to Central Africa in search of Dr. Livingston while in Africa

402
00:31:04,921 --> 00:31:08,420
Stanley tried taking down a hippopotamus with his rifle but his

403
00:31:08,421 --> 00:31:13,360
bullets bounced off the animals thick skin reports like this led Oliver

404
00:31:13,361 --> 00:31:16,960
Winchester to introduce even more powerful arms to his line of repeating

405
00:31:16,961 --> 00:31:23,440
rifles. The most famous of these was unveiled in 1873.

406
00:31:27,120 --> 00:31:34,900
This is the model 1873 Winchester. The basic improvement on this was a beefed

407
00:31:34,901 --> 00:31:41,680
up cartridge, a lot more power, and it was that Winchester's first centerfire

408
00:31:41,681 --> 00:31:46,660
round. They called it a central fire in those days because the primer was

409
00:31:46,661 --> 00:31:51,700
located in the center of the round rather than around the rim. The new

410
00:31:51,701 --> 00:31:56,320
mechanism fired cartridges that held one-third more black powder. With this

411
00:31:56,321 --> 00:32:01,540
improvement, the Winchester 1873 was capable of bringing down large game, like

412
00:32:01,541 --> 00:32:08,320
Buffalo, from 200 yards away. The rifle was now made of stronger materials, which

413
00:32:08,321 --> 00:32:11,120
made it more reliable under hard use.

414
00:32:11,121 --> 00:32:16,540
The model 1873 rifle incorporated several improvements.

415
00:32:16,541 --> 00:32:21,200
The first and most obvious is the change of the frame from brass to iron.

416
00:32:21,201 --> 00:32:27,240
A less obvious improvement that's part of that iron frame are removable side panels

417
00:32:27,241 --> 00:32:31,980
that allow someone to work on the toggle bolt mechanism without completely disassembling

418
00:32:31,981 --> 00:32:33,620
the gun.

419
00:32:33,621 --> 00:32:38,280
This was the legendary Winchester carried by nearly every character who plied their trade

420
00:32:38,281 --> 00:32:39,500
with a rifle in the West.

421
00:32:40,440 --> 00:32:42,040
Outlaw Billy the Kid's accuracy

422
00:32:42,041 --> 00:32:45,100
with a model 1873 Winchester is legendary.

423
00:32:45,920 --> 00:32:48,020
He is posed with one in the only photograph

424
00:32:48,021 --> 00:32:49,360
of him known to exist.

425
00:32:50,580 --> 00:32:52,960
The James brothers were known to have used them.

426
00:32:53,240 --> 00:32:55,080
Frank James commented that he relied

427
00:32:55,081 --> 00:32:57,980
on a Winchester rifle and a Remington revolver

428
00:32:57,981 --> 00:32:59,940
because the cartridges were interchangeable.

429
00:33:00,320 --> 00:33:02,720
And when a man makes his living running from the law

430
00:33:02,721 --> 00:33:05,460
or in such a desperate situation where gunfights

431
00:33:05,461 --> 00:33:08,140
and life-or-death struggles are imminent,

432
00:33:08,280 --> 00:33:13,580
He wants to make sure that he has firearms and ammunition that's readily available and reliable.

433
00:33:14,400 --> 00:33:19,080
Not surprisingly, lawmen were also quick to embrace the newest Winchesters.

434
00:33:20,640 --> 00:33:28,400
Texas Rangers was one of the first police organizations to wholeheartedly adopt the Winchester repeating rifle.

435
00:33:28,401 --> 00:33:34,380
They used model 1866s, then replaced them with model 1873s,

436
00:33:34,560 --> 00:33:38,400
and finally replaced the 1873s with model 1895s,

437
00:33:39,100 --> 00:33:42,760
which they carried well into the 20th century.

438
00:33:43,300 --> 00:33:45,180
As future models were introduced,

439
00:33:45,420 --> 00:33:50,000
the Winchester also became the favorite of a New Yorker-turned-rancher named Roosevelt.

440
00:33:51,340 --> 00:33:55,720
Theodore Roosevelt carried a number of different types of firearms

441
00:33:55,721 --> 00:34:00,680
and in his career as a hunter, as a marksman, as a soldier.

442
00:34:01,560 --> 00:34:09,960
He was very reluctant to let the world capitalize on the publicity

443
00:34:09,961 --> 00:34:13,300
that might be generated from the firearms that he used.

444
00:34:13,960 --> 00:34:18,780
He did not allow Winchester to capitalize on his name.

445
00:34:19,360 --> 00:34:22,780
But later, he complemented the Winchester in his memoirs.

446
00:34:22,781 --> 00:34:30,680
The Winchester is by all odds the best weapon I ever had, and I now use it almost exclusively.

447
00:34:31,120 --> 00:34:33,560
It is handy to carry, whether on foot or on horseback.

448
00:34:33,820 --> 00:34:38,640
It is absolutely sure, and there is no recoil to jar and disturb the aim.

449
00:34:39,240 --> 00:34:42,780
The Winchester is the best gun for any game to be found in the United States,

450
00:34:43,140 --> 00:34:48,380
and is unapproachable for the rapidity of its fire and the facility with which it is loaded.

451
00:34:48,381 --> 00:34:50,340
Theodore Roosevelt, 1885.

452
00:34:50,341 --> 00:34:55,860
Like Roosevelt, the Winchester was an American original.

453
00:34:56,740 --> 00:35:01,840
By the 1880s, Winchester had sold more than 100,000 lever-action rifles.

454
00:35:02,780 --> 00:35:08,220
Born of necessity, it grew up on the frontier and quickly became a cultural icon.

455
00:35:09,180 --> 00:35:11,520
Every cowboy and rancher carried it.

456
00:35:11,521 --> 00:35:14,780
They were preferred by hunters and sought after by sportsmen.

457
00:35:15,440 --> 00:35:18,960
Lady bandit Pearl Hart favored it for robbing stagecoaches in Arizona.

458
00:35:18,961 --> 00:35:25,960
Even children who were taught to shoot at a young age learned on a Winchester.

459
00:35:25,961 --> 00:35:36,960
But while the story of the West was nearing the end of its trail, the Winchester legend had only just begun.

460
00:35:36,961 --> 00:35:40,960
Life in the American West was a gritty existence at best.

461
00:35:40,961 --> 00:35:46,960
Comforts that were taken for granted back home were unavailable on the trail.

462
00:35:46,961 --> 00:35:50,400
Into this harsh environment came the Winchester Repeater.

463
00:35:50,401 --> 00:35:52,860
It was graceful in both line and form,

464
00:35:52,861 --> 00:35:55,300
a marvel of early industrial design.

465
00:35:57,120 --> 00:35:59,460
Historic because of its roles on the battlefield

